Lockpicking — Standard Pin Tumbler Guide
materials:
tension wrench (L-shaped, flat steel)
lock pick hook (standard hook, .025" thickness)
practice lock (clear acrylic recommended for learning)
steps:
prepare the lock
ensure you own the lock or have explicit permission
insert the tension wrench
place the short end into the bottom of the keyway
apply light rotational pressure in the direction the key turns
maintain steady tension — too much binds pins, too little lets them reset
probe with the hook
insert the hook above the wrench, slide to the back
identify the binding pin
one pin resists more than others — this is the binding pin
lift it slowly until you feel or hear a click
the driver pin clears the shear line; the key pin drops
repeat for each pin
move to the next binding pin, lift the same way
continue until all pins sit at the shear line
open the lock
the plug rotates freely when the last pin sets
practical safety notes:
only pick locks you own or have permission to pick
picking locks without permission is illegal in most jurisdictions
start with clear practice locks to see the mechanics
use light tension — heavy tension oversets pins

